== What the Affections Are

I. It may be asked: What are the affections of the mind?

I answer: the affections are nothing other than the more vigorous and strongly felt exercises of the inclination and will of the soul.

God has given the soul two faculties. One is that by which it is capable of perception and speculation, or by which it discerns and views and judges things; this is called the understanding. The other faculty is that by which the soul does not merely perceive and view things, but is in some way inclined with respect to the things it views or considers: it is either inclined #emph[to them,] or disinclined and averse #emph[from them.] In other words, it is the faculty by which the soul does not look on things as an indifferent, unaffected spectator, but either as liking or disliking them, pleased or displeased, approving or rejecting. This faculty is called by various names. It is sometimes called the #emph[inclination.] As it has respect to the actions that are determined and governed by it, it is called the #emph[will.] And the mind, with regard to the exercises of this faculty, is often called the #emph[heart.]

The exercises of this faculty are of two sorts. There are those by which the soul is carried out toward the things in view, in #emph[approving] of them, being pleased with them, and inclined to them. And there are those in which the soul opposes the things in view, in #emph[disapproving] them, being displeased with them, averse from them, and rejecting them.

And as the exercises of the inclination and will of the soul are various in their #emph[kinds,] so they are much more various in their #emph[degrees.] There are some exercises of being pleased or displeased, inclined or disinclined, in which the soul is carried only a little beyond a state of perfect indifference. And there are other degrees above this, in which the approval or dislike, the pleasure or aversion, is stronger. In these we may rise higher and higher, until the soul comes to act vigorously and perceptibly, and its actings have such strength that (through the laws of the union the Creator has fixed between soul and body) the motion of the blood and animal spirits begins to be perceptibly altered. From this there often arises some bodily sensation, especially about the heart and vital organs, which are the fountain of the fluids of the body. And from this it comes about that the mind, with regard to the exercises of this faculty, is called #emph[the heart,] perhaps in all nations and ages. And it is to be noted that it is these more vigorous and strongly felt exercises of this faculty that are called the #emph[affections.]

The #emph[will] and the #emph[affections] of the soul are not two faculties. The affections are not essentially distinct from the will, and they do not differ from the mere actings of the will and inclination of the soul except in the liveliness and the felt strength of their exercise.

It must be confessed that language is somewhat imperfect here, and the meaning of words is to a considerable degree loose and unfixed, not precisely limited by custom, which governs the use of language. In one sense the affection of the soul differs not at all from the will and inclination, and the will is never in any exercise any further than it is #emph[affected.] It is not moved out of a state of perfect indifference in any other way than by being #emph[affected] one way or another, and it does nothing beyond that. Yet there are many actings of the will and inclination that are not so commonly called #emph[affections.] In everything we do voluntarily there is an exercise of the will and inclination; it is our inclination that governs us in our actions. But all the actings of the inclination and will in all the common actions of life are not ordinarily called affections. Yet what are commonly called affections are not essentially different from them, but only in the degree and manner of their exercise. In every act of the will whatever, the soul either likes or dislikes, is either inclined or disinclined to what is in view. These acts are not essentially different from the affections of #emph[love] and #emph[hatred.] The liking or inclination of the soul to a thing, if it is in a high degree and is vigorous and lively, is the very same thing as the affection of #emph[love.] And the disliking and disinclination, if it is in a great degree, is the very same as #emph[hatred.] In every act of the will #emph[for,] or #emph[toward,] something not present, the soul is in some degree inclined to that thing, and that inclination, if it is in a considerable degree, is the very same as the affection of #emph[desire.] And in every degree of the act of the will in which the soul approves of something present, there is a degree of being pleased, and that pleasure, if it is in a considerable degree, is the very same as the affection of #emph[joy] or #emph[delight.] And if the will disapproves of what is present, the soul is in some degree displeased, and if that displeasure is great, it is the very same as the affection of #emph[grief] or #emph[sorrow.]

Such seems to be our nature, and such the laws of the union of soul and body, that there is never, in any case whatever, any lively and vigorous exercise of the will or inclination of the soul without some effect on the body, in some change in the motion of its fluids, and especially of the animal spirits. And on the other hand, by the same laws of the union of soul and body, the constitution of the body and the motion of its fluids may promote the exercise of the affections. Yet it is not the body but the mind alone that is the proper seat of the affections. The body of a man is no more capable of being really the subject of love or hatred, joy or sorrow, fear or hope, than the body of a tree is, or than the same body of a man is capable of thinking and understanding. As it is the soul alone that has ideas, so it is the soul alone that is pleased or displeased with its ideas. As it is the soul alone that thinks, so it is the soul alone that loves or hates, rejoices or grieves at what it thinks of. Nor are these motions of the animal spirits and fluids of the body anything that properly belongs to the nature of the affections, though in the present state they always accompany them. They are only effects or accompaniments of the affections, entirely distinct from the affections themselves and in no way essential to them. So a spirit without a body may be as capable of love and hatred, joy or sorrow, hope or fear, or other affections, as one that is united to a body.

The #emph[affections] and #emph[passions] are frequently spoken of as the same. And yet, in the more common use of speech, there is in some respect a difference. #emph[Affection] is a word that in its ordinary meaning seems to be somewhat wider than #emph[passion:] it is used for all vigorous, lively actings of the will or inclination, but passion for those that are more sudden, whose effects on the animal spirits are more violent, and in which the mind is more overpowered and less in its own command.

As all the exercises of the inclination and will are either in approving and liking, or in disapproving and rejecting, so the affections are of two sorts. There are those by which the soul is carried out to what is in view, cleaving #emph[to] it or #emph[seeking] it; and there are those by which it is averse #emph[from] it and #emph[opposes] it.

Of the first sort are #emph[love, desire, hope, joy, gratitude, delight.] Of the second kind are #emph[hatred, fear, anger, grief,] and the like, which there is no need now to stop and define one by one.

And there are some affections in which the two kinds of actings of the will just mentioned are combined. In the affection of #emph[pity,] for instance, there is something of the #emph[first kind] toward the person suffering, and something of the #emph[second] toward what he suffers. And so in #emph[zeal] there is high #emph[approval] of some person or thing, together with vigorous #emph[opposition] to what is thought to be contrary to it.
